    "question": "Company A is $20/share with $100 NI, 100 shares outstanding. Company B is $5/share with $50 NI, 100 shares outstanding.A buys B with 60% stock and 40% cash.Assume the 40% cash portion is funded with new debt at 10% pre-tax interest, a 20% corporate tax rate, and no synergies.What is the pro-forma ownership, and is the deal accretive? By how much?",
    "answer": "IB Vine Select Merger Model (M&A) Pro-forma ownership: Purchase price (no premium) = $5×100 = $500. 60% stock = $300 → new A shares = $300 / $20 = 15. 40% cash = $200 (funded by raising new debt) @ 10% pre-tax; 20% tax ⇒ after-tax interest = $16. Pro-forma shares = 100 + 15 = 115 → B holders own 15 / 115 ≈ 13%. Accretion calculation: Pro-forma NI = 100 + 50 − 16 = 134. Pre-deal EPS = 100 / 100 = $1.00. Pro-forma EPS = 134 / 115 ≈ $1.17. Accretion = (1.17 / 1.00 − 1) ≈ +17%. Why accretive: A trades at 20× P/E (5% earnings yield); you’re buying B at 10× and funding 40% with ~8% after-tax debt, so the blended yield > 5%.",
