    "question": "What are the effects of a Deferred Tax Liability (DTL) and a Deferred Tax Asset (DTA) in a merger model?",
    "answer": "Reported in Unspecified Interview Superday Merger Model (M&A) Deferred Tax Liability (DTL) Created when book income > taxable income (e.g., accelerated tax depreciation). Represents future tax payments. In M&A: Purchase accounting may create new DTLs from asset write-ups. Reduces net assets acquired, increasing goodwill. Deferred Tax Asset (DTA) Created when taxable income > book income (e.g., NOLs, accruals). Represents future tax benefits. In M&A: Target's DTAs (like NOLs) may have limited value due to Section 382 limitations. Valuation adjustment may be needed for unusable DTAs. In merger models, DTLs and DTAs affect purchase price allocation, goodwill calculation, and pro forma tax rate.",
